# Pause before an exceptional consequence

**Reference pattern.** These examples are simulated. They do not install Home Assistant screens or control a device.

Use an interruption only when a person is likely to make a serious mistake or take an action they cannot undo. Repeated warnings for ordinary actions teach people to dismiss them without reading.

## Use it sparingly

Deleting a specific backup can justify a pause. Choosing a colour palette, opening a room or changing a reversible dashboard setting cannot. Start with an ordinary explanation. Add an interruption only when the consequence and the evidence call for it (WG-051).

<%= example('nhs-interruption-backup') %>

## Tell a likely mistake from an invalid value

A schedule that ends before it starts may be meant to run overnight. Explain how you read it and let the person confirm or change it. A value the device does not support gets an inline error instead (WG-041). Do not use a warning to override a device's supported limits.

<%= example('nhs-interruption-overnight') %>

## Confirmation is not permission

An interruption confirms what the person intends. It does not sign them in or give them a capability. If Home Assistant rejects the action, or the integration does not support it, say so and offer a valid way back. Never add “Continue anyway” to a prohibited action.

<%= example('nhs-interruption-permission') %>

## Accessibility and content

Name the object and the consequence in the heading or the first line. “Are you sure?” makes people work out the context. Use labels such as “Delete this backup” and “Keep the backup” (WG-036). Keep a working Back route and the browser history. Do not offer two differently labelled exits that do the same thing.

For a modal version, use a native accessible dialog with a label. Keep long content scrollable and return focus to the trigger on cancel. Selecting outside the dialog must not confirm a destructive action. Escape cancels or closes without submitting. Do not reopen a dismissed dialog each time an entity state refreshes. See [Dialogs](../components/dialogs.html).

## Home Assistant boundary

Use the native authorised API for the operation. Check the target and the person's permissions again just before running it. Tie the confirmation to a stable target, not a list position that can change while the dialog is open. Stop repeat submissions while the request is pending, and show the confirmed result (WG-044).

The examples are simulated. They do not delete backups, change schedules or bypass access controls. Safety-critical device actions need their own supported product workflow. This pattern does not show that a hazardous action is appropriate.
